---
model: claude
prompt:
  schemaStrictness: repair
permissions:
  # Use the runtime's native auto approval: an implementation run executes
  # commands and edits files without stopping to ask, and each runtime's own
  # reviewer (Claude's auto mode, Codex's auto-review subagent) decides what
  # needs escalating. acceptEdits left codex exec on its read-only default
  # sandbox with no one to approve a write.
  mode: auto
  tools:
    # Questions go through todo_ask, which blocks inside the run until the
    # answer arrives; a native question would end the run instead.
    AskUserQuestion: deny
---
{{#if multiple}}You are implementing the {{count}} todo items listed below. Each is a separate task — implement ALL of them.{{else}}You are implementing the todo item below in a codebase.{{/if}}

{{{body}}}{{#if existingPlan}}---
## Approved Plan

A human has reviewed and approved this implementation plan. Follow it — do not re-derive a different approach. If a step is wrong or blocked, ask with the `todo_ask` tool instead of silently diverging.

{{{existingPlan}}}

{{/if}}---
## Instructions

1. Implement ALL todo items listed above
2. Investigate the codebase to understand the root cause of each issue
3. Implement fixes that address the underlying issues
4. Do NOT run git add or git commit — gavel manages commits automatically
5. Record anything a later reader needs on the TODO with the todo tools (`todo_comment`, `todo_update`) rather than in prose only you will see.

Your fixes should:
- Address root causes, not mask symptoms
- Follow existing code patterns and style
- Pass all verification tests (if specified)
- Be minimal and focused

If you are blocked on a decision only a human can make, call `todo_ask` with your questions and continue with the answers; never guess. Do not use AskUserQuestion.

## Result

End with the final StructuredOutput call carrying three separate top-level fields: `success` (true when the work is done), `event` (`completed` when you implemented it, `blocked` when you could not), and `comment` (markdown describing what you did, found, or what blocked you; it becomes the run's history entry). Never JSON-encode an object or another field value into a string.
